INT. PRINTING SHOP, MANCHESTER - EVENING (1838)

Ink-black presses. Racks of lead type. A single tallow candle gutters beside a chalkboard of tomorrow's handbills.

EZEKIEL FARTHING, thirties, compositor, in a leather apron, sets type with quick, practised fingers. Ink stains him to the elbow.

A knock. The door opens, letting in cold and the smell of coal smoke.

PRUDENCE ASHWORTH, twenties, a mill-girl in a patched shawl, steps in clutching a folded paper.

PRUDENCE
Mr. Farthing? They said you'd print for a penny.

EZEKIEL
Depends what it says.

PRUDENCE
It says we want ten hours. No more.

EZEKIEL
Who is "we"?

PRUDENCE
The women at Greaves Mill. All three hundred.

EZEKIEL
And who signs it?

PRUDENCE
Nobody. That's the point. If nobody signs it, nobody's turned out.

Ezekiel wipes his hands on his apron. He takes the paper and reads it by the candle. His lips move.

EZEKIEL
It's a good bill.

PRUDENCE
Will you print it?

EZEKIEL
I'll print it. Come back Thursday.

PRUDENCE
Thursday's too late. The vote is tomorrow.

EZEKIEL
Then I'd best set it tonight.
